  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:  \nThe primary challenge is optimizing a non-convex, high-dimensional function under constraints. Gradient-based methods may get stuck in local minima, while standard evolutionary algorithms lack directed search. The proposed approach combines **simulated annealing** with **gradient-based local refinement** and **multi-resolution exploration**. Simulated annealing allows escaping local minima via stochastic tunneling, while gradient-based refinement accelerates convergence. Multi-resolution exploration balances between coarse-grained and fine-grained representations to leverage complementarity of scales.\n\nKey contributions:  \n- **Novel Repair Mechanism**: Uses a hierarchical repair strategy to handle constraints.  \n- **Adaptive Gradient Projection**: Projects gradients onto feasible polytopes for efficient movement.  \n- **Multi-resolution Anchoring**: Starts from the parent solution (560 points) then explores variants at double resolution (1120 points) to exploit finer separations.  \n\nThis approach addresses computational bottlenecks by decoupling coarse topology optimization (via annealing) from fine-grained polishing (via gradient projection). The hierarchical repair ensures physical interpretability while the two-level resolution scheme allows for systematic exploitation of scale-dependent structures.\n\nMathematical Specification:  \n**Variables**:  \n- Discretized function \\(h(x_i) \\in [0,1]^n\\) with \\(x_i \\in [0,2]^n\\), \\(\\sum h(x_i) = n/2\\)  \n- Shifted target: \\(1-h(x+k)\\)  \n**Objective**:  \n\\[\\min_{h} \\max_{k=-n+1}^{n-1} dx \\cdot \\text{Overlap}(k) \\quad \\text{with} \\quad dx=2/n\\]  \n**Constraints**:  \n\\[0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\quad \\forall i, \\quad \\sum h_i = n/2\\]  \n\nImplementation Procedure:  \n1. **Hierarchical Repair**:  \n   - Project onto [0,1]^n: \\(h_i' = \\min(\\max(h_i, 0), 1)\\)  \n   - Correct sum:  \n     \\[h_C = h_P + \\lambda \\cdot (n/2 - \\sum h_P) \\cdot sgn(h_C - 0.5)\\]  \n     \\[\\text{where } sgn() \\text{ ensures minimal distortion}\\]  \n     Iteratively clip and scale until convergence.\n\n2. **Objective Function**:  \n   \\[f(h) = \\max_k \\left| \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\\text{'full'}) \\right| \\cdot (2/n)\\]\n\n3. **Simulated Annealing**:  \n   - Initialize: \\(h_0\\) (parent or random repair)  \n   - Cooling schedule: \\(T(t) = T_0 \\exp(-t/T_\\text{cycles})\\)  \n   - Move generation:  \n     \\[h_{t+1} = h_t + \\delta_t \\cdot g_t\\]  \n     \\[g_t = \\text{proj}_{\\partial \\mathcal{F}}(h_t) / \\| \\text{proj}_{\\partial \\mathcal{F}}(h_t) \\|\\]\n     where \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) is feasible polytope.\n\n4. **Gradient Projection Method**:  \n   - Use finite-differences for gradient estimate:  \n     \\[\\nabla f(h) \\approx \\frac{f(h + \\epsilon) - f(h)}{\\epsilon} + \\nabla \\text{const\\_constraint}(h)\\]\n   - Project gradient onto \\(\\partial \\mathcal{F}}\\) and apply step size \\(\\alpha_t\\) decaying exponentially.\n\n5. **Resolution Adaptation**:  \n   - Base resolution: \\(n = \\text{parent length}\\)  \n   - Double resolution phase:  \n     \u2022 Upsample original \\(h\\) by replication  \n     \u2022 Trim to \\(n^\\prime = 2n\\)\n     \u2022 Apply identical optimization algorithm  \n     \u2022 Merge back to base resolution by averaging windows\n\n6. **Termination Criteria**:  \n   - \\(\\|\\nabla f(h)\\| < \\epsilon\\)  \n   - \\(n_\\text{sanneal} \\cdot n_\\text{local} > \\max(\\text{budget}/0.005, 10^6)\\)\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:  \n- **Annealing schedule variation**: Logarithmic cooling (for APPROVED)  \n- **Gradient estimation**: Central finite-differences (preferred) or Broyden (fallback)  \n- **Resolution strategy**:  \n  1. Base optimization  \n  2. 50% time for resolution doubling  \n  3. Hierarchical merging  \n- **Repair variation**: Penalty-based (quadratic) vs. hierarchical clipping  \n\nComputebudget and Robustness:  \n- **Dominant Cost**: ~50% runtime for interpolation (base to double resolution)  \n- **Memory**: Two resolution levels simultaneously stored (~2\u00d7 memory)  \n- **Failure recovery**: Hierarchical repair preserves connectivity  \n- **Deadline heuristics**:  \n  \u2022 Warm-up: 20% annealing phase  \n  \u2022 Cooling progression: step-size \\(\\alpha_t = 1 - (t/T_\\text{max})^\\beta\\) (\\(\\beta=0.5\\))  \n  \u2022 Objective threshold: stops improvements (\\(\\delta f < 10^{-6}\\))  \n\nValidation and Return Contract:  \n1. **Objective Quality**:  \n   - Final objective evaluated with parent's resolution  \n   - Multiple restarts (5-10) triggered by lack of improvement  \n2. **Constraint Verification**:  \n   - Repair applied post-initiation and final output  \n   - Hierarchical clip monitors [0,1] (max violation < \\(\\epsilon\\))  \n3. **Output Format**:  \n   ```python\n   return (np.array(h_final), evaluate_final(h_final), n_base)\n   ```\n   where \\(n_\\text{base}\\) matches parent's resolution.  \n   Additional reparametrization ensures \\(\\sum h_{\\text{final}} = n/2 \\pm 10^{-10}\\).  \n\nCompatibility with Libraries:  \n- `numpy.clip` for projections  \n- `scipy.optimize.line_search` fallback  \n- `time.time()` for cooling schedule  \n- `np.random.normal` for stochastic moves  \n\nFallback Handling:  \n- Hierarchical representation ensures that even if double resolution fails, base solution is always compiled.  \n- Repair mechanism guarantees \\(0.9_{95}\\%\\) constraint satisfaction.  \n- Centrifugal random walk variant prevents only local entrapment.\n\nEnd-of-Budget Behavior:  \n- Objectives evaluated at double resolution (1120 points)  \n- Best solution stored (memory-bound)  \n- When budget tight: prioritizes annealing (global search)  \n\nFinal Check:  \n- C5 value must satisfy \\(C_5 \\leq 0.3808\\)  \n- Implemented with repair constraints and repair mechanisms.",
